content.text:
I have everything needed. Here are the findings.

## 1. The real add-to-cart click handler — exact call chain

The click originates in the base-pattern `renderActionButtons()` inside `overrides/app/components/product-view/index.tsx` (this project's copy-first port of the base `product-view`; the PDP imports it via a **relative** path specifically so the override resolver doesn't redirect it back to base — see the comment at `overrides/app/pages/product-detail/index.tsx:55-62`).

`overrides/app/components/product-view/index.tsx:525-555`:
```tsx
const handleCartItem = async () => {
  const hasValidSelection = validateAndShowError();

  if (!hasValidSelection) {
    return null;
  }

  if (!addToCart && !updateCart) return null;
  if (updateCart) {
    await updateCart(variant || product, quantity);
    return;
  }
  try {
    const itemsAdded = await addToCart!([{ product, variant, quantity }]);
    if (itemsAdded) {
      onAddToCartModalOpen({
        product,
        itemsAdded,
        selectedQuantity: quantity,
      });
    }
  } catch (e) {
    showError();
  }
};
```
This is wired to the button at `product-view/index.tsx:570-587`:
```tsx
<Button
  key="cart-button"
  onClick={handleCartItem}
  isDisabled={disableButton}
  isLoading={isBasketLoading}
  width="100%"
  variant="solid"
  marginBottom={4}
>
```

`ProductView`'s `addToCart` prop is `handleAddToCart` from `overrides/app/pages/product-detail/index.tsx:1049` (`addToCart={handleAddToCart}`), whose body is at **`product-detail/index.tsx:507-598`**. The actual mutation call is a **single line**, using the helper — not hand-composed raw mutations:

```tsx
const basketResponse = await addItemToNewOrExistingBasket(productItems);
```

That helper comes from `useShopperBasketsMutationHelper()` (aliased from `useShopperBasketsV2MutationHelper`) at `product-detail/index.tsx:205`:
```tsx
const { addItemToNewOrExistingBasket } = useShopperBasketsMutationHelper();
```

**Answer: it calls the helper's `addItemToNewOrExistingBasket`, not raw `useShopperBasketsMutation('createBasket')`/`'addItemToBasket'` composed by hand in this file.** (Raw `useShopperBasketsMutation('updateItemsInBasket')` is used separately, only for the bundle-child-variant follow-up PATCH at line 741-752 — a second call made *after* the item is already in the basket, not part of the initial add.)

The raw `createBasket`/`addItemToBasket` mutations ARE used by hand, but in a different file/purpose: `product-view/index.tsx:321-322` uses them only for `prepareBasket()` (Express/Buy-Now payments' *temporary* basket), not for the real Add-to-Cart click path.

The helper itself — `node_modules/@salesforce/commerce-sdk-react/hooks/ShopperBasketsV2/helpers.js:39-87` — is what actually composes the raw mutations:
```js
function useShopperBasketsMutationHelper() {
  const customerId = useCustomerId();
  const { data: basketsData } = useCustomerBaskets({parameters: {customerId}}, {enabled: !!customerId});
  const createBasket = useShopperBasketsMutation('createBasket');
  const addItemToBasketMutation = useShopperBasketsMutation('addItemToBasket');
  return {
    addItemToNewOrExistingBasket: async function (productItem) {
      if (basketsData && basketsData.total > 0) {
        const currentBasket = basketsData?.baskets && basketsData.baskets[0];
        return await addItemToBasketMutation.mutateAsync({
          parameters: { basketId: currentBasket.basketId },
          body: productItem
        });
      } else {
        const data = await createBasket.mutateAsync({ body: {} });
        if (!data || !data.basketId) { throw Error('Something is wrong. Please try again'); }
        return await addItemToBasketMutation.mutateAsync({
          parameters: { basketId: data.basketId },
          body: productItem
        });
      }
    }
  };
}
```

## 2. Required input shape

`handleAddToCart` builds the array itself (`product-detail/index.tsx:509-518`):
```tsx
let productItems: BasketLineItem[] = productSelectionValues.map((item) => {
  const { variant, quantity } = item;
  const prod = variant || item.product || product;
  return {
    productId: prod?.productId || prod?.id, // productId for variant, id for product
    price: prod?.price,
    quantity,
  };
});
```
So each item needs `productId` (**variant's `productId` if a variant is selected, else the master product's `id`**), `price`, and `quantity` — plus whatever `addInventoryIdsToPickupItems`/`getShipmentIdForItems` later merge in (`inventoryId`, `shipmentId`) for pickup/multiship. This is called from `product-view.tsx:541`: `addToCart!([{ product, variant, quantity }])`.

Confirmed against the SDK's real `ProductItem` body type (`node_modules/commerce-sdk-isomorphic/lib/index.esm.d.ts:6968-6994`): `productId`, `quantity`, `price`, `inventoryId`, `shipmentId`, `bundledProductItems`, and `optionItems` are all valid fields. **This codebase's real add-to-cart code never sets `optionItems`** — there's no configurable/option-product handling anywhere in this flow (only `bundledProductItems`, for the separate bundle path). If your `useProduct`/`useProductSearch` result includes a selected variant, use `variant.productId`; otherwise fall back to `product.id`.

## 3. Guest's first add-to-cart / no-basket handling

**Handled automatically inside the helper — no explicit "create basket if none exists" branch needed by calling code.** The `if (basketsData && basketsData.total > 0) {...} else { createBasket... then addItemToBasket... }` branch shown above is entirely internal to `addItemToNewOrExistingBasket`.

`useCurrentBasket` (`node_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/hooks/use-current-basket.js`) **is** called in `product-detail/index.tsx:204` (`const { data: basket, isLoading: isBasketLoading } = useCurrentBasket();`), but it's used only to read the *existing* basket for pickup/multiship/shipping-method-conflict checks (`basket?.shipments`, `basket.productItems?.length`) — it plays no role in creating the basket. Basket creation is entirely `addItemToNewOrExistingBasket`'s own internal `useCustomerBaskets` check, a separate query call inside the helper.

## 4. Error/success feedback — the toast pattern

**Important correction to the prompt's premise:** `overrides/app/hooks/use-toast.ts` **does not exist in this repo.** `protectedFiles.md:85` lists it under "Shared Hooks — 5+ consumers" as a Tier 2 path, but `ls overrides/app/hooks/` shows no such file — that entry appears aspirational/stale, not a real override. Both real call sites import `useToast` straight from base:
```tsx
import { useToast } from '@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/hooks/use-toast';
```
(`product-detail/index.tsx:109`, `product-view/index.tsx:68`). The base implementation (`node_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/hooks/use-toast.js`) wraps Chakra's `useToast`, returning a function taking `{title, description, status, action, position, duration, variant, isClosable}`.

The real usage pattern for add-to-cart specifically, in `product-detail/index.tsx:487-496`:
```tsx
const showToast = useToast();
const showError = (errorMessage?: unknown) => {
  const errorText = typeof errorMessage === 'string' ? errorMessage : formatMessage(API_ERROR_MESSAGE);
  showToast({
    title: errorText,
    status: 'error',
  });
};
```
called on failure at line 596: `showError(error instanceof Error ? error.message : undefined);` inside `handleAddToCart`'s `catch`.

There is **no success toast for add-to-cart** in this codebase — success instead opens the add-to-cart modal (`onAddToCartModalOpen(...)` in `product-view.tsx:546-550`), via `useAddToCartModalContext`. The wishlist flow (same PDP file, `handleAddToWishlist`, lines 452-467) is the only place a success toast is used, with an action button. If your demo wants a toast on success (rather than the modal), that wishlist success-toast call is the closest real precedent to copy; for failure, `showError`'s pattern above is exactly what's already used for the real add-to-cart failure path — reuse `showToast({title, status: 'error'})` (or `'success'`) verbatim.

## 5. Guest-safe? Yes — fully transparent, no branch

`useCustomerId()` (`node_modules/@salesforce/commerce-sdk-react/hooks/useCustomerId.js`) returns whatever ID is in local storage/auth context, guest or registered, with **zero type distinction at the call site**. Nothing in `handleAddToCart`, `handleCartItem`, or the helper branches on guest-vs-registered.

This works because SLAS guest login happens automatically underneath, and — critically — every mutation call transparently awaits a valid token before firing, regardless of UI-visible `customerId` state. `useAuthorizationHeader.js:38-41` (used by every `useShopperBasketsMutation` call via `useMutation.js:31-32`):
```js
var _ref = _asyncToGenerator(function* (options) {
  const { access_token } = yield auth.ready();
  ...
```
`auth.ready()` resolves once a guest-or-registered token exists (`node_modules/@salesforce/commerce-sdk-react/auth/index.js` handles `loginGuestUser`/`loginGuestUserPrivate` automatically when there's no session). So even a click that races ahead of `customerId` being populated in state is safe — the mutation itself blocks on `auth.ready()` first. This confirms guest-vs-logged-in is handled transparently, not via a specific branch in the PDP/product-view code — directly relevant to this project's "Magento passwords not migrated" constraint.

## 6. Loading/pending UI on the real button

The real button (`product-view/index.tsx:570-587`, quoted in full above) uses:
- `isDisabled={disableButton}` — a **validation/stock** gate (`showInventoryMessage` / set-bundle child orderability), computed at lines 363-398, unrelated to mutation state.
- `isLoading={isBasketLoading}` — **this is not the add-to-cart mutation's pending state.** `isBasketLoading` is threaded down from `product-detail/index.tsx:204`: `const { data: basket, isLoading: isBasketLoading } = useCurrentBasket();` — i.e., it reflects whether the *existing basket query* is still fetching, not whether `addItemToNewOrExistingBasket` is currently in flight. **There is no wiring anywhere in this real flow from the mutation's own `isPending`/`isLoading` to the button.** (`useShopperBasketsMutation` returns a standard TanStack Query v4 mutation object — confirmed via `@tanstack/react-query@4.43.0` and `useMutation.js:32` — so `.isLoading` is available on `addItemToBasketMutation`/`createBasket` if you want it, but the codebase's real PDP simply doesn't use it for this button.)

## Bonus finding — directly affects your demo page plan

`ZimmProductCard`'s `home` variation **does not expose `onAddToBag` at all**, and explicitly hides the bag button:

`overrides/app/components/ui-kits/organisms/zimmProductCard/index.tsx:198-201`:
```tsx
export type ZimmProductCardHomeVariation = {
  variation: 'home';
  // No additions — Home never shows the bag, attributes, or size chips (see VARIATION_CONFIG).
};
```
